s("Which way should a Cornelius owner sell on a tight schedule?",
"<p>You can hire an agent, sell by owner, or take an as-is cash purchase. The pick depends on your closing deadline and on how much work the house needs before strangers tour it.</p>"
"<p>Census data lists a median owned-home value of $559,400 for the town, so a month of carrying costs or a failed financing contingency matters. A listing brings staging, photos and showings, then a wait for the buyer's lender. Cash buyers can accept a closing date that you pick.</p>"
"<ul><li>Agent listing: suited to a move-in-ready house and a flexible calendar.</li>"
"<li>Sale by owner: keeps the commission, adds disclosures and negotiating to your plate.</li>"
"<li>Cash purchase: suited to inherited houses, deferred repairs or a looming deadline.</li></ul>"
"<p>The deed is filed with the Mecklenburg County Register of Deeds. state recording law makes the registration moment, not the signing date, the point of priority over creditors and later purchasers.</p>")
s("What is the town of Cornelius like?",
"<p>Cornelius is a Lake Norman town in northern Mecklenburg County. The Census Bureau's July 1, 2025 estimate is 35,094 residents, and the town describes itself as the second youngest of the county's six incorporated towns.</p>"
"<p>The town website dates its founding to 1893 and its incorporation to March 4, 1905, and traces the origin to a squabble over cotton weighing in neighboring Davidson. One cotton firm moved just south of Davidson, built a mill with money from Joseph Benjamin Cornelius, and the town took his name. In 1963 Duke Power dammed the Catawba River to form Lake Norman, which the town credits with roughly 70 miles of lakeside property. It also lists ten public parks and a championship golf course.</p>"
"<table><caption>Cornelius in Census QuickFacts</caption><thead><tr><th>Topic</th><th>Cornelius</th></tr></thead><tbody>"
"<tr><td>Residents, July 2025 estimate</td><td>35,094</td></tr>"
"<tr><td>Residents, 2010 Census</td><td>24,866</td></tr>"
"<tr><td>Age 65 or older</td><td>20.0%</td></tr>"
"<tr><td>Homes occupied by their owners</td><td>69.5%</td></tr>"
"<tr><td>Owned-home value, typical</td><td>$559,400</td></tr>"
"<tr><td>Typical gross rent per month</td><td>$1,722</td></tr>"
"<tr><td>Household income, median</td><td>$113,767</td></tr>"
"<tr><td>Land (2020)</td><td>12.64 square miles</td></tr></tbody></table>"
"<p>With a fifth of residents past 65, state tax relief for older owners matters to many local sellers and heirs.</p>")
s("How will Mecklenburg County's 2027 revaluation affect a Cornelius sale?",
"<p>The county revalues every four years, and its tax office says the next one lands in 2027 with values set as of January 1, 2027. Notices and updated online records are due in early 2027, and the uniform schedule of values went to the commissioners on September 1, 2026.</p>"
"<p>County staff say elected officials adjust rates after a revaluation to keep revenue neutral unless they choose otherwise. Value times rate gives the bill, and the county, the town and Charlotte each set their own rates. A buyer who sees a high new value may worry about the bill, so point them to the county's online property record and the town's rate decision.</p>"
"<ul><li>Informal review: ask for it soon after your notice arrives, since the county allows 30 days.</li>"
"<li>Formal appeal: presented to the county's Board of Equalization and Review.</li>"
"<li>Next step: the state Property Tax Commission, after the board mails its decision.</li></ul>"
"<p>The county says staff spend about two years visiting properties, checking recorded features, comparing sales and noting improvements before values are set.</p>")
s("When do Cornelius tax bills fall due, and when does interest begin?",
"<p>The bill is due September 1 for the fiscal year it covers, and paying before January 6 avoids any interest. Interest of 2 percent applies to January 6 through the end of January, followed by three quarters of a percent for each month or part month until paid.</p>"
"<p>A county or town can offer a discount for early payment if it adopts a schedule by May 1 before the due date, so ask the Mecklenburg tax office whether one applies. Mailed payments count from the postmark date.</p>"
"<p>The lien reaches back to the January listing date. Ask the county tax collector for the status of the current bill so the closing attorney can pay it from your proceeds.</p>")
s("What relief might a Cornelius homeowner claim?",
"<p>State law has three options, and one owner may use only one of them. Applications go to the county assessor, not the Department of Revenue.</p>"
"<ul><li>Older or disabled owners: the greater of $25,000 or half the appraised value is excluded, subject to an income limit NCDOR prints as $38,800 for 2026.</li>"
"<li>Disabled veterans: $45,000 of value is excluded, with no age or income limit on the NCDOR form.</li>"
"<li>Circuit breaker: the bill is capped at a percentage of income, with a $58,200 ceiling for the higher bracket in 2026.</li></ul>"
"<p>Use Form AV-9, accepted through June 1 before the tax year. Cornelius adds one local feature: the town's FAQ says a designated historic landmark gets an automatic deferral of half the ad valorem tax on the designated portions. A structure needs to be at least 50 years old to start, and the Charlotte-Mecklenburg Historic Landmarks Commission handles eligibility.</p>")
s("What does a Cornelius seller pay to record the deed?",
"<p>The seller owes the deed excise tax, paid to the register's office before filing. State law sets it at a dollar for every $500 of the price, with any remainder rounded up.</p>"
"<p>The county and state split it evenly. Recording fees are a separate statewide schedule that adds a charge for every page past the first 15 on an ordinary instrument, and a satisfaction of an old deed of trust records free.</p>"
"<p>Your payoff, any unpaid tax and the attorney's fee come out of the proceeds, so ask for a draft settlement statement early.</p>")
s("Which Cornelius permit and zoning rules affect a buyer?",
"<p>Mecklenburg County issues building permits, but the Cornelius Planning Department signs off on zoning first. The town's FAQ warns that unpermitted work must be disclosed at sale and can raise your taxable square footage.</p>"
"<ul><li>Accessory dwelling: one per property, in the rear yard, and with other accessory structures it may reach half the principal building's footage at most. Travel trailers and RVs do not count.</li>"
"<li>Short-term rental: called transient occupancy and allowed only in areas on the town's Transient Occupancy Map, under Chapter 6.2.43 of the Land Development Code.</li>"
"<li>Watershed: most of town lies in the Lake Norman or Mountain Island watershed, where impervious-area caps and water quality buffers can apply.</li></ul>"
"<p>A buyer who wants a nightly rental should check the map before offering, and a lakefront buyer should ask about buffers.</p>")
s("What do the Census numbers say about Cornelius buyers?",
"<p>The town has 13,884 households averaging 2.36 people, and 85.5 percent of residents lived in the same house a year earlier. Per capita income is $77,539, poverty stands at 5.4 percent, and 96.9 percent of households subscribe to broadband.</p>"
"<p>Education runs high: 62.2 percent of adults 25 and older hold a bachelor's degree or more, and the mean commute is 25.4 minutes. Veterans number 1,560. Density rose from 2,058.4 residents per square mile in 2010 to 2,485.9 in 2020, on about 12.64 square miles of land.</p>"
"<p>QuickFacts lists 2022 retail sales of 854,773 thousand dollars, or $26,217 per resident, and accommodation and food services sales of 106,549 thousand dollars.</p>")
s("What paperwork should I gather before a Cornelius sale?",
"<p>Put these documents in one folder before you speak with buyers.</p>"
"<ul><li>Deed, mortgage payoff letter and the latest county tax bill.</li>"
"<li>Recorded plat, since the town says setbacks start with the plat on file at the Register of Deeds.</li>"
"<li>County permit history for decks, sheds and additions.</li>"
"<li>Any lease or rental registration you hold.</li></ul>"
"<p>The town's FAQ notes that a recorded plat is the first place to look for setback information, and that the map book and page appear in your deed of trust.</p>")
s("What if the Cornelius house is inherited, in foreclosure or rented?",
"<p>Each situation runs on a state timetable, and a cash purchase can often fit within it. Keep every notice and note its date.</p>"
"<ul><li>Foreclosure: a clerk of superior court holds the hearing, and a later upset bid must beat the last bid by at least 5 percent, with a $750 floor.</li>"
"<li>Inherited home: an estate of $20,000 or less in personal property may use an affidavit once 30 days pass, and North Carolina repealed its estate tax for deaths from 2013 onward.</li>"
"<li>Tenants: a month-to-month tenancy ends on 7 days' notice.</li></ul>"
"<p>Ask a North Carolina attorney which timetable fits your papers.</p>")
